{"t":1793375793,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"2e8e14ba-da36-43de-b5ff-c9eda14306dd","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-30T13:47:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 301347\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n947 AM EDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nNo significant changes to the forecast for today.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Very warm conditions today, with seasonable temperatures an dry \nconditions for Halloween weekend. \n\n- 2) Rain chances will likely hold off until the second half of next \nweek.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Very warm conditions today, with seasonable \ntemperatures an dry conditions for Halloween weekend. \n\nLatest satellite and observations indicate a weak cold front is\nbeginning to enter the area from the northwest. Clouds over the\nAlleghenies are dissipating late this morning as dry air advects\nin, so expect mostly sunny skies across the region. The \ngradient between the departing front and incoming high pressure \nwill yield west to northwest downsloping winds up to 25 mph. \n\nExpect above normal temperatures in the low to mid 70s (60s \nmountains). Locations south of DC and down toward \nCharlottesville have a shot to briefly touch 80 degrees given \nthe very dry air ahead of and behind the front. \n\nHigh pressure will builds across the region into the weekend,\nbringing cooler temperature. Expect much more seasonable \nweather for both Halloween Saturday and Sunday. Highs both days \nwill push into the 60s with overnight lows in the 30s and 40s. \nExpect more sunshine Saturday compared to Sunday given the high \nnearby. Cloud cover will increase Sunday afternoon and night as \na shortwave trough passes south of the area. This trough may \ntouch off a shower or two Sunday night although most will remain\ndry given the residual mid-level dry air with high pressure \nnearby. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain chances will likely hold off until the second \nhalf of next week.\n\nModels are arriving at better agreement that mid level ridging will \nbuild along the east coast during the first half of next week. A \nshortwave trough may cross the area Monday before the ridge takes \nhold, and there is still an outside chance a few showers could occur \nas it passes. At a minimum, expect cloudier conditions. Temperatures \nwill likely climb Tuesday and Wednesday, with highs reaching the 70s \nfor many areas.\n\nA deep trough will approach from the west the second half of the \nweek. A low pressure system will likely be lurking over the Bahamas, \nand some guidance suggests this system, or at least some of its \nmoisture, could be swept northward into the Mid Atlantic ahead of \nthe trough. While the details remain uncertain, expect increasing \nrain chances in the Wednesday through Friday period.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /13Z FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nPrevailing VFR conditions are forecast to continue through the \nupcoming weekend as high pressure builds into the region. A dry cold \nfront will cross the terminals later this morning and into the \nafternoon leading to some passing mid and high level cloud cover. \nWinds will kick up in the wake of the front out of the west and \nnorthwest with gusts between 20 to 25 kts. Highest gusts will be at \nterminals near the waters and along the ridges. Winds will gradually \ndiminish Saturday morning into Saturday afternoon as high pressure \nsettles overhead. Northerly winds will prevail through Saturday \nnight at less than 10 kts. \n\nVFR conditions are expected Sunday into Monday as high pressure \ncontinues to meander nearby. Winds will turn to the south and \nsoutheast and less than 15 kts. Significant weather is unlikely \nMonday and Tuesday as high pressure moves eastward. However, some \nlow chances of sub-VFR ceilings exist Monday or Monday night \ndepending on how the weather pattern evolves.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nA weak/dry cold front will push across the waters this morning with \nhigh pressure building south over the region this weekend. SCA \nconditions are expected from late this morning through early \nSaturday morning, especially over the open waters. This is largely \ndue in part to northerly channeling. Winds diminish below SCA level \nSaturday morning before falling back below sub-SCA levels Saturday \nafternoon. Sub-SCA conditions look to continue Sunday into Monday \nwith wedging high pressure meandering nearby. Winds will change to \nthe south and southeast as high pressure sits north and east of the \narea. Speeds will generally be between 5-10 kts. Showers may impact \nthe middle and southern waters Monday afternoon as a wave of low \npressure approaches from the south and west. Better chances for \nrain by the end of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nOffshore flow develops behind a cold frontal passage this morning \nallowing tidal anomalies to drop over the next few days. Winds turn \nback to the south Sunday afternoon leading to modest rises in \nanomalies heading into early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory until 9 AM EDT Saturday for ANZ530>543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...ADS/CJL/EST/KRR\nAVIATION...ADS/EST\nMARINE...ADS/EST\n"},"forecast":{"t":1793375725,"days":{"2026-10-30":{"hi":76,"lo":45,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-31":{"hi":63,"lo":45,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-01":{"hi":66,"lo":45,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Cloudy"},"2026-11-02":{"hi":67,"lo":48,"pop":2,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-03":{"hi":72,"lo":53,"pop":2,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-04":{"hi":73,"lo":57,"pop":34,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-11-05":{"hi":72,"lo":55,"pop":49,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"}}},"grid":{"t0":1793372400,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,n